  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A cold frontal passage today will bring a few \nto several hour period of showers in most places, followed by a\nweekend cooldown and a frost/freeze risk early next week\n\nOur recent warm spell (with daytime high temps some 8 to 12 deg\nF above normal and nighttime low temperature departures even\ngreater) will exit to the east as clouds and the chance for\nshowers increase today with the arrival of a cold front. \n\nPrior to the cold frontal passage (across the NW Mtns late this\nmorning, and elsewhere across the Central Mtns and Lower Susq\nValley this afternoon and evening respectively) max temps will\nonce again come in well above normal (+10-15 deg F) over the SE\nhalf of the state and still near normal across the Northwest. \n\nTemperatures will become seasonably cooler this weekend into \nnext week, when another stronger cold front will push through\nSunday night and early Monday. \n\nThe second surge of the cooldown will be highlighted by frost \npotential across the western and central Alleghenies Monday and \nTuesday night with min temps near the freezing mark across the \nnorthern tier. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2: Not much rain expected for the first 7-10 days \nof October \n\nWe'll have a few, generally limited opportunities for rain over\nthe next 7+ days in CPA. The first will come this morning over \nthe Northwest Mtns as cold front over the Lower Great Lakes \ndrifts gradually southeast. \n\n02/00Z High Res model members and the HREF are a little more \nrobust with rainfall amounts across the CWA today (compared to\nthe 12Z Thu run) and even ramp up the intensity of the showers \nacross the SE third of the state with a potential for TSRA in \nthe 20+ percent range given some small areas of MU CAPE values \nof up to 750 J/KG during the mid to late afternoon with temps in\nthe L80s and sfc Tds in the mid 60s.\n\nThe second opportunity for rain is on Sunday focused mainly \nover the southern and eastern portions of the CWA. High \nconfidence in a dry stretch for much of next week as large area \nof Canadian high pressure dominates the pattern.",
